description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:130-143 declares:

    /// Format 32 raw digest bytes as a canonical lowercase-hex
    /// [Sha256]. Infallible because every byte produces two
    /// lowercase-hex nibbles by construction.
    pub fn from_bytes(b: &[u8; 32]) -> Self { ... }

The docstring does not specify byte ordering. The implementation processes b[0] first and emits its high nibble first, then low nibble — i.e. the wire hex string places b[0] in positions 0-1, b[31] in positions 62-63. This matches FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 output convention (most significant byte first), which is what the sha2, ring, and openssl crates all produce when finalising a SHA-256 hasher.

But it is implicit. For a wire-format crate that explicitly disclaims carrying any SHA-256 *computation* (lib.rs and the digest.rs module preamble both say \"servers / consumers compute the digest themselves\"), the byte-order convention IS part of the public contract. A consumer who has digest output from an unusual source — a hardware HSM that emits little-endian, a tape-archive format that pre-reverses, a custom test vector format — will silently produce wrong wire values.

Suggested rustdoc addition:

    /// Format a 32-byte SHA-256 digest into the canonical lowercase-hex
    /// [Sha256] wire shape. The input is the digest as produced by a
    /// standard FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 implementation (sha2, ring, openssl):
    /// the most-significant byte first, with b[0] occupying positions
    /// 0-1 of the resulting hex string and b[31] occupying positions
    /// 62-63. Infallible because every byte produces two lowercase-hex
    /// nibbles by construction.

The NIST FIPS 180-4 test vector already in tests (digest.rs:267-278) anchors the convention, but a contract claim belongs in the rustdoc, not the test. A doc test cross-referencing the sha2 or ring API output would tie this down further.
